;; What was measured on 2026-09-09 while attempting the whole-component build,
;; and what was NOT. The `:blocked` disposition recorded for this tranche rests
;; on a gap table; most of that table does not reproduce.
{:as-of "2026-09-09"
 :amu-which-amu
 "First measured against orgs/kotoba-lang/amu, which carried 584 uncommitted
  files from a concurrent session -- a working tree, not the pin. That caveat
  is now LIFTED for everything below: the numbers were re-measured in a clean
  worktree of amu at origin/main 3ef1a709, which runs bin/amu once
  node_modules is linked in from the sibling checkout (node_modules is not in
  git, which is the whole reason a clean worktree looked unable to run). The
  two trees disagree only where they should -- the same component compiles to
  the same 5577 bytes but `main` sits at offset 636 under the older tree and
  628 under the pin, because the pins below it moved."

 :constructs-the-gap-table-called-blocking
 ;; Each is one minimal probe under `amu check --jvm-free`, probes in
 ;; ../../q9-probes and reproducible.
 {:string-literal            :pass    ; (defn label [] :string "hello")
  :string-concat             :pass    ; the head is string-concat, not str
  :string=                   :refuted ; corrected below: the head is string=?
  :string-substring          :pass
  :count-over-list           :pass    ; [:list :i64]; [:vector ...] is not the type
  :vector-of-maps-constant   :refuted ; corrected below: passed only unused
  :map-constant              :pass}

 :capability-imports
 ;; ADR-q9: a clock or an entropy source crosses as a DECLARED CAPABILITY
 ;; IMPORT and is not a reason to keep a component in Clojure. Both of this
 ;; component's mechanisms are reachable.
 {:clock/now    {:wire-id 7  :status :pass}
  :entropy/draw {:wire-id 23 :status :pass}
  :form "(ns … (:capabilities #{:clock/now :entropy/draw})) then (clock/now x)"
  :policy "{:allow #{[:cap/call 7] [:cap/call 23]}}"
  :note "amu refuses a capability declared but never called, and refuses a
         call the policy does not grant. Both refusals were observed."}

 :built
 {:check   {:command "amu check --jvm-free --policy policy.edn" :result :ok}
  :compile {:command "amu compile --target aarch64-macos --policy policy.edn"
            :result :ok :bytes 5577}}

 :executed
 ;; A build is not acceptance. This is the execution.
 ;; The artifact is EDN; `extract-native` lifts one exported symbol out of it
 ;; as raw code, and `tools/kexe_loader.c` maps and calls it.
 ;;   amu compile … --output wc.kexe
 ;;   amu extract-native wc.kexe --symbol <sym> --output <sym>.bin
 ;;   cc -std=c11 -O2 tools/kexe_loader.c -o kexe-loader
 ;;   ./kexe-loader <sym>.bin <offset> <arity> aarch64 7,23 [i64 ...]
 {:host "aarch64 (aarch64-macos), 2026-09-09"
  :main {:call "(page-limit 250)" :native 100 :oracle 100}

  ;; aave.main/page-limit: (if (pos? r) (min r max-limit) default-limit),
  ;; max-limit 100, default-limit 20. Eight inputs, native == oracle on all.
  :page-limit {-5 20, 0 20, 1 1, 20 20, 99 99, 100 100, 101 100, 250 100}
  :clamp-limit {0 20, 100 100, 101 100}

  ;; aave.main/has-more-kernel?: (> remaining limit). 1 = true, 0 = false.
  :has-more? {[99 100] 0, [100 100] 0, [101 100] 1}

  :boundaries-that-discriminate
  ;; The comparison rule: a check that owns an operator needs an input ON the
  ;; line, or `>` and `>=` are the same program.
  {:page-limit-at-0
   "requested = 0 answers 20. Under `>=` the first test would take the other
    arm and answer 0. This input separates the two operators; 100 does not
    (both arms answer 100 there)."
   :has-more-at-100-100
   "remaining = limit answers 0. Under `>=` it answers 1."}

  :capability-calls
  {:now-at        {:wire 7  :result 1}
   :new-id-suffix {:wire 23 :result 1}
   :control
   "The same two calls with the grant withdrawn (`kexe-loader … -`) do not
    return a different number -- they trap: KEXE_TRAP {:kind :signal :signal
    :SIGTRAP}, exit 120. So the grant is doing something, measured in both
    directions rather than assumed from a green run."}}

 :the-internal-compiler-error-was-mine
 ;; Correcting the claim this file carried when it was first committed.
 {:claimed
  "`amu extract-native --symbol main` answers `internal compiler error` on
   this component, on amu's own examples/todo-app.kotoba, and on a minimal
   `(defn main [] :i64 42)` -- therefore not component-specific, therefore a
   compiler defect blocking the tranche."
  :actual
  "`extract-native` does not read source. It reads the EDN artifact that
   `compile --output` writes. All three inputs were .kotoba SOURCE files. One
   caller error, made three times and read as three data points for one
   conclusion. Handed the artifact instead, the same command answers :ok true
   and the code it lifts runs (above)."
  :the-part-that-is-a-real-defect
  "The command answered `internal compiler error`, exit 70 -- the code
   reserved for the compiler breaking -- for a caller naming the wrong file,
   and discarded the underlying `No protocol method IAssociative.-assoc
   defined for type object: (ns m (:export [main]))` on the way. That is the
   8 questions' third (do not discard the error body) in the form
   ADR-2609081600 names: a refusal that HAD a decidable reason and declined to
   decide it. Fixed in amu as `source-path/admit-native-artifact!`, which
   decides both halves -- the extension, which is a closed contract that
   namespace already owns, and the keys extract-native is about to read.
   Reverted, the new test goes from 0 failures to 6."
  :what-it-cost
  "About an hour, and a committed finding that named the wrong blocker for
   nine other repos. The measurement that would have caught it immediately is
   the one the skill already prescribes: run the artifact. I reported a build
   as blocked without ever having a build to run."}

