 :re-measured-2026-09-09
 ;; Claims this file and com-aadhaar's migration-record.edn carried, checked
 ;; against amu at this worktree's pin. A claim is only listed if it was run.
 {:vector-of-maps-constant-was-a-false-pass
  "This file said `:vector-of-maps-constant :pass` on the strength of
   q9-probes/p-vecmaps.kotoba, which DEFINES the vector and never uses it. Use
   it -- q9-probes/p01-vecmaps-used.kotoba adds (count specs) -- and it is
   refused: `map literal with keyword keys carries i64 values; the value at
   key :entity is :string. A keyword-keyed literal lowers to the legacy
   untagged pair map ... write (typed-map-new [:map :keyword :string] ...)`.
   The entity-specs def that src/aave/whole_component.kotoba carried until
   today was therefore dead code that would not have compiled if anything had
   read it: an unused definition is not type-checked, so the probe and the
   component were green for the same wrong reason."
  :string=-is-not-the-head
  "This file said `:string= :pass`. It is not a head at amu's pin of
   kotoba-sema (96fd4e19): `unknown operation: string= is not a builtin, a
   sugar head, or a function of this module; nearest defined: string=?
   (builtin), string-byte-length (builtin), string-code-point-at (builtin)`.
   Probe: q9-probes/p03-string-heads.kotoba. scripts/kotoba-admitted-
   vocabulary.cljs prints string= and string-length because it reads the
   SUPERPROJECT's kotoba-sema checkout, which is a different sha from the one
   amu compiles against. The vocabulary tool is a guide; amu is the authority."
  :integer-to-string-exists
  "amu's own examples/todo-app.kotoba says in its header that Kotoba has no
   integer-to-string builtin, and builds item keys out of a letter table
   because of it. string-from-i64 is a head and reaches native:
   q9-probes/p02-string-from-i64.kotoba, check 0, native 0. That header is a
   correct record of the day it was written and a stale constraint now; this
   component's RFC-3339 rendering depends on the head existing."
  :typed-maps-are-admitted-and-my-first-four-probes-were-caller-error
  "q9-probes/p04..p09 record a sequence in which typed maps looked refused at
   check time. They were not: typed-map-get takes (type value key), and those
   probes passed (map key default) in Clojure's shape, so the MAP was being
   validated as a type descriptor and `value type is outside the safe profile`
   was the honest answer to the program actually written. Corrected, every
   key/value pair tried is admitted at check. They are refused by the aarch64
   backend, which is a different fact and is recorded in
   :the-carrier-measurement. The probes are kept failing as written because
   the sequence is the point."
  :com-aadhaar-gap-table
  "com-aadhaar/migration-record.edn (:as-of 2026-09-03, amu 57ba0ee0) lists as
   REJECTED: string literals anywhere, (count v), (reduce f 0 xs), (case x),
   (min a b), map literals, vector-of-maps constants. Re-measured at this
   pin: string literals pass, count over [:list :i64] passes, reduce passes,
   case on a keyword passes, min passes amu check (and is refused by the
   aarch64 backend under `aggregate ABI rejected: call-abi-not-admitted`,
   which is a target fact and not a subset fact). The vector-of-maps constant
   is genuinely refused when used, as above. That record's whole-component
   attempt is a transliteration of main.cljc -- untyped defn parameters, str,
   parse-long, trim, mapv, reduce-kv, and calls to persist!/query which the
   file never defines -- so its :kotoba/source-read-failed is a fact about
   that file and not about the language. NOTE: the com-aadhaar checkout read
   here is at 73bac8ca, which is NOT the west pin 560d7444 for that path, and
   the pin is not an ancestor of it."}

